## Your Task

Classify each changed file into exactly **one** of the following groups based on its primary focus:

| Group | Name | File characteristics |
|-------|------|---------------------|
| 1 | Correctness | Models, services, controllers, business rules, domain logic, algorithms, data transformations, core library code, orchestration logic |
| 2 | Security & Reliability | Auth modules, middleware, API routes/handlers, input validation, encryption, external API clients, webhook handlers, OAuth/SSO, rate limiting, CORS config |
| 3 | Quality & Style | UI components, templates, views, stylesheets, frontend code, formatters, display logic, i18n/l10n files |
| 4 | Coverage & Performance | Test files, benchmarks, performance-critical paths (database queries, caching, batch processing), config files, CI/CD, build scripts, Dockerfiles |

**Default rule:** Files that do not clearly match any group go to Group 1 (Correctness).

**Tie-breaking:** Use the filename and directory as the primary signal. For example, `auth_service.py` goes to Group 2, `UserCard.tsx` goes to Group 3, `test_auth.py` goes to Group 4. When still ambiguous, prefer the group matching the file's dominant concern.

## Output Format

Return your classification as structured text in exactly this format:

```
### Group 1: Correctness
- path/to/file1.py
- path/to/file2.py

### Group 2: Security & Reliability
- path/to/file3.py

### Group 3: Quality & Style
- path/to/file4.tsx

### Group 4: Coverage & Performance
- path/to/test_file.py
```

Rules:
- Every file from the changed files list must appear in exactly one group.
- If a group has no files, still include the group header with no files listed.
- Do not add files that are not in the changed files list.
- Do not include any other text outside of this format.
